  "problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=8.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha =8\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$",
